[h1]Lizarb Interface[/h1]

A complete reskin of RimWorld 1.6's interface. Window frames, buttons, tabs, tooltips, checkboxes, gizmos, scrollbars, fonts and the Architect menu.

[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/dequevedo/lizarb-interface/main/docs/titles/Presets.png[/img]

Pick a preset and the whole interface changes at once: the textures, the typeface and its sizes, the text outline, the background pattern and its strength, and how the Architect menu is coloured.

[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/dequevedo/lizarb-interface/main/docs/themes.png[/img]

A preset is not just a skin. Two presets can draw with the same textures and read completely differently, which is what separates PixelStone from PixelStone Fake: the same pixels, one with a pixel typeface and no background, one without.

Change anything and save it as a preset of your own. The texture sets are grouped: hand painted ones, and the Legacy set a script drew.

The first entry is [b]RimWorld default[/b], which hands every surface and the font back to the game.

[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/dequevedo/lizarb-interface/main/docs/titles/Fonts.png[/img]

Change the font the whole game is drawn in. The picker draws every name in its own face, so you pick by looking. Anything installed on your machine shows up too.

[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/dequevedo/lizarb-interface/main/docs/fonts.png[/img]

Text sizes are adjustable, and every label can carry a real outline.

[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/dequevedo/lizarb-interface/main/docs/titles/Settings.png[/img]

The settings cover shapes for the Architect colour, shown here, plus the opening animation, the gap between elements, the strength of the background texture, whether small text gets an outline, and a switch for each surface the mod touches.

[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/dequevedo/lizarb-interface/main/docs/shapes.png[/img]

[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/dequevedo/lizarb-interface/main/docs/titles/TheArchitectmenu.png[/img]

In the Architect menu, category buttons get a colour and an icon, so you find the one you want by sight instead of by reading. Categories from other mods get one too. Pick the shape, or turn it off.

[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/dequevedo/lizarb-interface/main/docs/architect.png[/img]

[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/dequevedo/lizarb-interface/main/docs/titles/Compatibility.png[/img]

For compatibility with another UI mod, load this one last if you want its look to win.

Buttons, window frames, tabs, widgets and scrollbars can each be handed back to the game on their own, so a clash costs one checkbox instead of the whole skin.

[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/dequevedo/lizarb-interface/main/docs/titles/BuiltwithAI.png[/img]

This mod was built with AI. The code and this description were written with Claude, Anthropic's assistant, with me directing, testing and deciding.

No image AI made the art. The skins grouped as Legacy were drawn by a script from shapes and maths. The hand painted ones are drawn by me, and that is where new work goes.

[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/dequevedo/lizarb-interface/main/docs/titles/Sourceandlicence.png[/img]

The source and its licence are on GitHub:

[url=https://github.com/dequevedo/lizarb-interface]github.com/dequevedo/lizarb-interface[/url]

Code and textures are GPL-3.0, and the full source ships inside the mod folder. The fonts are under the SIL Open Font License, with each licence file included and every designer credited.

The icons come from the [url=https://nieobie.itch.io/free-icons]Game Icon Pack v1.4 by Nieobie[/url], released under CC0.

[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/dequevedo/lizarb-interface/main/docs/titles/Dependencies.png[/img]

